%T A396681 3,2,2,2,4,3,3,2,4,2,4,2,2,2,5,2,4,3,2,3,3,3,3,2,2,3,2,3,2,4,2,3,2,2,
%U A396681 3,2,3,4,2,2,2,5,3,2,4,2,5,2,3,2,2,3,3,2,3,2,3,4,2,3,2,4,3,3,2,2
%N A396681 Number of iterations for the n-th prime to reach 2 under the function f(p) = smallest odd prime factor of 5*p+1 or 2 if 5*p+1 is a power of 2.
%C A396681 The function f_5 is defined as follows: f_5(2)=2. For p>2, compute N = 5p+1. Remove all powers of 2 to obtain the odd part m. If m=1, then f_5(p)=2. Otherwise, f_5(p) is the smallest prime factor of m. Equivalently, define f_5(1)=2 to cover the case where the odd part is 1. The function f_5 belongs to the family f_b(p) = smallest odd prime factor of the odd part of b*p+1. For b=5, convergence to 2 has been computationally verified for all primes up to 10^9.
%H A396681 Jose O. del Toro Barroso, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18796391">Una función iterativa sobre números primos que siempre termina en 2</a> (An iterative function on prime numbers that always ends in 2), Zenodo, 2026.
%e A396681 a(2)=1 because the second prime, 3, goes to 2 in one step: 5*3+1=16=2^4, so f_5(3)=2.
%e A396681 a(4)=2 because the fourth prime, 7, follows the trajectory 7 -> 3 -> 2, taking 2 steps.
%p A396681 b:= proc(n) option remember; (s-> `if`(s={}, 0,
%p A396681       b(5*min(s)+1)+1))({ifactors(n)[2][.., 1][]} minus {2})
%p A396681     end:
%p A396681 a:= n-> b(ithprime(n)):
%p A396681 seq(a(n), n=1..100);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Jun 02 2026
%t A396681 f[n_] := Module[{m = 5*n + 1}, FactorInteger[m/2^IntegerExponent[m, 2]][[1, 1]]]; a[n_] := -1 + Length[NestWhileList[f, Prime[n], # > 2 &]]; Array[a, 100] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Jun 02 2026 *)
%Y A396681 Cf. A395740 (stopping times for k=3).
%K A396681 nonn
%O A396681 1,3
